===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To produce one or more [[copy|copies]] of a text or image on a [[surface]], especially by machine.
#: {{hyponyms|en|print out|print off}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Print''' the draft double-spaced so we can mark changes between the lines.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Richard Collett|title=He ran out of countries to visit, so he created his own|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/06/08/travel/slowjamastan-micronation|date=2023-06-08|passage=Two years on, and while the Sultan of Slowjamastan has instigated more than a few bizarre laws (he’s outlawed the wearing of Crocs, for example), the Republic also has all the trappings of a fledgling nation-state. It issues its own passports, flies its own flag, '''prints''' its own currency (“the duble”), and has a national anthem that’s played on state occasions.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260516010746/https://www.cnn.com/2023/06/08/travel/slowjamastan-micronation|archivedate=16 May 2026}}
# To produce a [[microchip]] (an [[integrated circuit]]) in a process resembling the printing of an image.
#: {{ux|en|The circuitry is '''printed''' onto the semiconductor surface.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[write]] very [[clearly]], especially, to write without [[connecting]] the [[letters]] as in [[cursive]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Print''' your name here and sign below.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm only in grade 2, so I only know how to '''print'''.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[publish]] in a [[book]], [[newspaper]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|How could they '''print''' an unfounded rumour like that?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1716|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=The Poetical Works of Alexander Pope|section=Preface|text=From the moment he '''prints''', he must expect to hear no more truth.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To stamp or impress (something) with coloured figures or patterns.
#: {{ux|en|to '''print''' calico}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To fix or impress, as a stamp, mark, character, idea, etc., into or upon something.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1547?|author=w:Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey|title=Description of the Fickel Affections, Pangs, and Slights of Love|text=A look will '''print''' a thought that never may remove.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1629|author=w:Sir John Beaumont|title=Bosworth Field|text=Upon his breastplate he beholds a dint, / Which in that field young Edward's sword did '''print'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1701|author=w:Wentworth Dillon, 4th Earl of Roscommon|title=Silenus|text=some footsteps '''printed''' in the clay}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To stamp something in or upon; to make an impression or mark upon by pressure, or as by pressure.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Palamon and Arcite
|passage=Forth on his fiery steed betimes he rode, / That scarcely '''prints''' the turf on which he trod.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang}} To [[inadequately]] [[conceal]] a [[weapon]] such that its [[outline]] or [[imprint]] is visible on the person wearing it.
# {{lb|en|computing|transitive}} To display a [[string]] on the [[terminal]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Chuck Easttom|title=Advanced JavaScript|publisher=Jones & Bartlett Learning|isbn=9781449633486|page=217|passage=However, when you '''print''' the string you can see only 11 characters (c, a, r, &#39;, s,, w, h, e, e, l).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Othmar Kyas|title=How To Smart Home: A Step by Step Guide to Your Personal Internet of Things|publisher=Key Concept Press|isbn=9783944980065|passage=On the RHS side we write the current date to the variable date and '''print''' it to the terminal window, followed by the string &quot;Chris coming home....&quot; .}}
# {{lb|en|finance|ambitransitive}} To produce an observable value.
#: {{ux|en|On [[w:2020 stock market crash#16–20 March|March 16, 2020]], the [[S&P]] '''printed''' at 2,386.13, one of the worst drops in history.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fingerprint]] (a person).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Eric Lustbader|title=Pale Saint|page=24
|passage=Maybe we'll get lucky; maybe he was '''printed''' for some minor infraction in some backwater town.}}

